{"title":"Photosynthesis: How Plants Make Food","key_idea":"Plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to make their own food and release oxygen.","method":["Identify the three things plants need: sunlight (energy), water (from soil), and carbon dioxide (from air)","Understand that plants mix these ingredients together inside their leaves","Remember that the food made is glucose, which helps the plant grow","Know that oxygen is released back into the air as a waste product"],"worked_example":{"problem":"A plant sits on a sunny windowsill with soil and water. What does it need to make food?","steps":["The plant gets sunlight from the window","Water comes from the soil through the roots","Carbon dioxide enters the leaves from the air","The plant combines all three to make glucose"],"answer":"Sunlight, water from soil, and carbon dioxide from air"},"key_terms":[{"term":"Photosynthesis","meaning":"process where plants make food using sunlight"},{"term":"Glucose","meaning":"the food that plants make"},{"term":"Carbon dioxide","meaning":"gas from the air that plants use"},{"term":"Oxygen","meaning":"gas that plants release back into the air"}],"common_mistakes":["Thinking plants eat food from the soil like we do—they make their own food","Forgetting that plants need air (carbon dioxide) as well as water and light"],"remember":"Sunlight + Water + Carbon dioxide = Glucose + Oxygen","quick_check":[{"q":"Name the three things a plant needs to make food.","a":"Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide"},{"q":"What do plants release into the air during photosynthesis?","a":"Oxygen"}],"tell_your_child":"A plant is like a little food factory that uses sunshine to cook its own meals! It takes in water from the ground and air from around us, and makes yummy glucose to grow big and strong."}
```
